{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Why do you ask for my name?</b> You do not need to ask my name. It is the way of the world: when travelers who have never seen each other meet, they are accustomed at the time of their parting to exchange words of affection and friendship, and they ask each other, 'What is your name?' — so that if you send a messenger from yourself who mentions your name, I will fulfill your request. So too Manoah said to the angel: 'What is your name, that when your word comes we may honor you?' And so Jacob did with the angel, but the angel replied to him: you have no need for that. Another interpretation: 'Why do you ask for my name?' — you do not need to ask my name; angels do not wish to reveal their names, lest people adjure them. And so we find with Manoah: 'Why do you ask for my name, and it is wonderful?' Another interpretation: 'Your name you asked' — to change it according to the deed; 'but my name' — what would it avail you, why do you ask it of me?"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The second interpretation's closing phrase והוא פלאי is rendered as 'and it is wonderful,' following Judges 13:18 which Manoah's question alludes to; the word may also be read adjectivally meaning 'it is wondrous.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:11.995368882Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}